<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-14-nominals" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Nominals</title>

  <objectives>
    <title>Learning Objectives</title>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Distinguish between nouns (form) and nominals (function/role in a sentence)</p></li>
      <li><p>Identify the various structures that can function as nominals</p></li>
      <li><p>Understand the six syntactic positions nominals occupy</p></li>
      <li><p>Analyze gerund phrases, infinitive phrases, and complement clauses as nominals</p></li>
      <li><p>Distinguish between that-clauses and wh-clauses as complement clauses</p></li>
      <li><p>Compare gerund, infinitive, and clausal nominal structures</p></li>
      <li><p>Key terms: Noun (form), Nominal (function), Gerund, Gerund phrase, Infinitive phrase (nominal), Complement clause, That-clause, Wh-clause, Extraposition, Nominalized adjective</p></li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <p>Chapter 12 introduced adverbials—structures that modify verbs, adding information about time, place, manner, and reason. Chapter 13 introduced adjectivals—structures that modify nouns, describing or restricting what those nouns refer to. With this chapter, the triad is complete. <term>Nominals</term> are structures that fill the argument slots of a sentence: the subject, the direct object, the indirect object, the object of a preposition, and the subject complement. Together, these three functional categories—adverbial, adjectival, nominal—account for nearly every role a phrase or clause can play in English.</p>

  <p>What makes nominals surprising is the range of forms that can fill a position you might expect only a noun to occupy. Consider the subject slot: in <foreign>Happiness is rare</foreign>, a single noun fills it. But in <foreign>That she persisted surprised everyone</foreign>, an entire clause with its own subject and verb fills the same slot. In <foreign>Swimming every morning changed his life</foreign>, a phrase built on a verb form does the same. This chapter explains how noun phrases, pronouns, gerund phrases, infinitive phrases, and complement clauses can each step into the noun's shoes—and why recognizing them matters for both analysis and writing.</p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 14.1  NOUNS VS. NOMINALS                                      -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="ch-14-nominals-sec-141">
    <title>Nouns vs. Nominals: Form vs. Function</title>

    <p>You have already encountered the form-vs.-function distinction twice: in Chapter 12, where you saw that adverbs, prepositional phrases, noun phrases, and clauses can all serve the adverbial function; and in Chapter 13, where you saw that adjectives, nouns, participial phrases, and relative clauses can all serve the adjectival function. Here the same logic applies to the nominal function. The form of a word tells you its part of speech; the function tells you the role it plays in a sentence. A word or phrase that fills a noun-phrase slot—regardless of its internal structure—is functioning nominally.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Noun as Form</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>A <term>noun</term> is a part of speech—a word class with particular morphological and syntactic characteristics (Chapter 5). Nouns can be pluralized, possess, follow determiners, and serve as subjects and objects. Examples: <foreign>book, happiness, Maria, government</foreign>.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Nominal as Function (Role in a Sentence)</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>A <term>nominal</term> is any word, phrase, or clause that functions in a position typically occupied by a noun phrase—as subject, object, or complement. The key insight: all nouns functioning as subjects or objects are nominals, but not all nominals are nouns.</p>

    <p>Consider a noun phrase filling the subject role:</p>

    <image source="diagrams/new/ch14_table_008.png" width="65%">
      <description>Multi-level labeling table for "The students read books"</description>
    </image>
    <image source="diagrams/new/ch14_np_subject.png" width="55%">
      <description>Syntax tree for "The students read books" showing a noun phrase as subject</description>
    </image>
    <p><c>[S [NP [DET The] [N students]] [VP [V read] [NP [N books]]]]</c></p>

    <p>Now compare a gerund phrase filling the same subject role:</p>

    <image source="diagrams/new/ch14_table_009.png" width="65%">
      <description>Multi-level labeling table for "Reading books is fun"</description>
    </image>
    <image source="diagrams/new/ch14_gerund_subject.png" width="55%">
      <description>Syntax tree for "Reading books is fun" showing a gerund phrase as subject</description>
    </image>
    <p><c>[S [NOM [V Reading] [NP [N books]]] [VP [V is] [ADJP [ADJ fun]]]]</c></p>

    <p>Both <foreign>The students</foreign> and <foreign>Reading books</foreign> fill the subject slot. Their forms are completely different—a noun phrase and a gerund phrase—but their function is the same.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>The Six Nominal Positions</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>Unlike adverbials (which are optional) and adjectivals (which modify nouns), nominals fill the <em>argument</em> positions of a clause—the slots that the verb requires or that the sentence structure provides. Chapter 8 introduced these positions; here is a quick reference:</p>

    <tabular halign="left">
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell>Position</cell>
        <cell>Location</cell>
        <cell>Example</cell>
      </row>
      <row><cell>Subject</cell><cell>Before the verb</cell><cell><foreign><em>The dog</em> barked.</foreign></cell></row>
      <row><cell>Direct Object</cell><cell>After a transitive verb</cell><cell><foreign>She read <em>the book</em>.</foreign></cell></row>
      <row><cell>Indirect Object</cell><cell>Before the direct object</cell><cell><foreign>She gave <em>me</em> a book.</foreign></cell></row>
      <row><cell>Object of Preposition</cell><cell>After a preposition</cell><cell><foreign>We talked about <em>the problem</em>.</foreign></cell></row>
      <row><cell>Subject Complement</cell><cell>After a linking verb</cell><cell><foreign>She is <em>a doctor</em>.</foreign></cell></row>
      <row><cell>Object Complement</cell><cell>After the direct object</cell><cell><foreign>They elected her <em>president</em>.</foreign></cell></row>
    </tabular>

    <p>See Section 14.3 for a fuller treatment of these positions with examples from all nominal types.</p>

  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 14.2  STRUCTURES THAT FUNCTION AS NOMINALS                    -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="ch-14-nominals-sec-142">
    <title>Structures That Function as Nominals</title>

    <p>The simplest nominal is what you would expect: a noun phrase with a determiner, perhaps some modifiers, and a noun at its head. But the nominal function is not restricted to noun phrases. Pronouns, complement clauses, gerund phrases, infinitive phrases, and even some adjectives can all occupy the same syntactic slots. The catalog below moves from the most typical to the more structurally complex, so you can see the full range of what English treats as equivalent to a noun phrase. Use the following table as a quick reference; each form is treated in greater depth in the sections that follow.</p>

    <table xml:id="ch-14-forms-at-a-glance">
      <title>Nominal Forms at a Glance</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="minor" left="minor">
        <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell>Form</cell>
          <cell>Internal Structure</cell>
          <cell>Example</cell>
        </row>
        <row><cell>Noun phrase (NP)</cell><cell>Determiner + (Modifiers) + Head Noun</cell><cell><foreign>The interesting book</foreign> arrived.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Pronoun</cell><cell>Single word; carries case</cell><cell><foreign>She</foreign> arrived.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Gerund phrase</cell><cell><foreign>-ing</foreign> verb + (Object/Modifiers)</cell><cell><foreign>Reading novels</foreign> is fun.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Infinitive phrase</cell><cell><foreign>to</foreign> + verb + (Object/Modifiers)</cell><cell><foreign>To win</foreign> was her goal.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>That-clause</cell><cell><foreign>that</foreign> + Subject + Predicate</cell><cell>I know <foreign>that she left</foreign>.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Wh-clause</cell><cell>wh-word + (Subject) + Predicate</cell><cell>I wonder <foreign>where she went</foreign>.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Nominalized adjective</cell><cell>Determiner + Adjective</cell><cell><foreign>The poor</foreign> need help.</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>1. Noun Phrases</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>The prototypical nominal is a noun phrase:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>The book</em> is on the table.</foreign> (subject)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>I read <em>the interesting book</em>.</foreign> (direct object)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>This is <em>my sister</em>.</foreign> (subject complement)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>2. Pronouns</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>Pronouns replace entire noun phrases and fill all the same positions:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>She</em> arrived early.</foreign> (subject)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>I saw <em>them</em>.</foreign> (direct object)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>She gave <em>me</em> a book.</foreign> (indirect object)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>3. Gerund Phrases</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>The <foreign>-ing</foreign> form of a verb (gerund) heads phrases that function as nominals:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>Swimming</em> is good exercise.</foreign> (subject)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>She enjoys <em>reading novels</em>.</foreign> (direct object)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>She succeeded by <em>working hard</em>.</foreign> (object of preposition)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>4. Infinitive Phrases</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>Infinitive phrases (<foreign>to</foreign> + verb) can function as nominals:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>To err</em> is human.</foreign> (subject)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>She wants <em>to succeed</em>.</foreign> (direct object)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>The goal is <em>to finish on time</em>.</foreign> (subject complement)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>5. That-clauses (Complement Clauses)</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>Clauses introduced by <foreign>that</foreign> function as nominals. These are also called <em>complement clauses</em> or <em>nominal clauses</em>:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>That she resigned</em> surprised everyone.</foreign> (subject)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>I believe <em>that she is honest</em>.</foreign> (direct object)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>The problem is <em>that we're out of time</em>.</foreign> (subject complement)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>6. Wh-clauses (Complement Clauses)</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>Clauses introduced by wh-words also function as nominals:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>What she said</em> was important.</foreign> (subject)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>I don't know <em>where he went</em>.</foreign> (direct object)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>The question is <em>who will lead</em>.</foreign> (subject complement)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>7. Nominalized Adjectives</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>Some adjectives, with a determiner, function nominally by referring to a group of people:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>The poor</em> need assistance.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>The accused</em> denied the charges.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 14.3  NOMINAL POSITION                                        -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="ch-14-nominals-sec-143">
    <title>Nominal Position</title>

    <p>One of the most useful things about the nominal function is that it gives you a systematic way to identify what role any noun-like structure is playing in a sentence. Rather than asking <q>Is this a noun?</q>—a question about form—you ask <q>What slot does it fill?</q>—a question about function. The answer is one of six positions: subject, direct object, indirect object, object of a preposition, subject complement, or object complement. Each position has a characteristic location in the sentence, and recognizing the position tells you immediately how the nominal relates to the verb and the rest of the clause.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Subject Position</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>The subject sits before the main verb and names what the sentence is about—the entity performing the action or being described. <em>Diagnostic:</em> ask <q>who or what is doing or being something?</q> The answer is the subject. Across the four examples below, notice that the form changes from a single noun phrase to an entire embedded clause, but the role stays the same:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>The students</em> studied.</foreign> (NP)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>That he won</em> surprised us.</foreign> (that-clause)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>Swimming</em> is fun.</foreign> (gerund)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>To forgive</em> is divine.</foreign> (infinitive)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Direct Object Position</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>The direct object follows a transitive verb and receives the action—it is the thing acted upon. <em>Diagnostic:</em> isolate the verb, then ask <q>verb what?</q> or <q>verb whom?</q> The answer is the direct object. Most verbs that take an object accept several different nominal forms in this slot, though some restrict their objects to a particular form (see Section 14.10).</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>She read <em>the book</em>.</foreign> (NP)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>I know <em>that she's coming</em>.</foreign> (that-clause)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>He enjoys <em>playing chess</em>.</foreign> (gerund)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>She wants <em>to succeed</em>.</foreign> (infinitive)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Indirect Object Position</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>The indirect object sits between the verb and the direct object and identifies the recipient or beneficiary of the action. <em>Diagnostic:</em> ask <q>to whom?</q> or <q>for whom?</q>—the answer is the indirect object. This position is more restricted than the others; it is filled almost exclusively by noun phrases and pronouns, with wh-clauses appearing only occasionally:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>She gave <em>me</em> a book.</foreign> (pronoun)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>Tell <em>whoever asks</em> the truth.</foreign> (wh-clause)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Object of Preposition</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>The object of a preposition completes a prepositional phrase. <em>Diagnostic:</em> find a preposition (<foreign>about, in, by, for, with</foreign>, etc.); whatever nominal follows and completes its meaning is the object of the preposition. Of all six positions, this one shows the sharpest restriction: gerunds and noun phrases appear here freely, but infinitive phrases cannot fill this slot at all (you cannot say <foreign><delete>interested in to learn</delete></foreign>).</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>We talked about <em>the problem</em>.</foreign> (NP)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>I'm interested in <em>what you said</em>.</foreign> (wh-clause)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>She succeeded by <em>working hard</em>.</foreign> (gerund)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Subject Complement Position</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>A subject complement follows a linking verb (<foreign>be, seem, become, appear</foreign>) and renames or describes the subject. <em>Diagnostic:</em> after a linking verb, ask <q>the subject equals what?</q>—if a nominal answers that question, it is functioning as the subject complement. Unlike direct objects, subject complements do not name a different entity; they identify the subject from another angle:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>She is <em>a doctor</em>.</foreign> (NP)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>The problem is <em>that we're late</em>.</foreign> (that-clause)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>His hobby is <em>collecting coins</em>.</foreign> (gerund)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>The goal is <em>to finish on time</em>.</foreign> (infinitive)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Object Complement Position</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>An object complement follows the direct object and renames or characterizes <em>the direct object</em>—not the subject. It appears after verbs like <foreign>elect, name, call, consider, declare, appoint</foreign>, where one entity is being assigned an identity, role, or label. <em>Diagnostic:</em> after the direct object, ask <q>the direct object equals what?</q>—if a nominal answers that, it is functioning as the object complement:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>They elected her <em>president</em>.</foreign> (NP)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>We named the dog <em>Rex</em>.</foreign> (NP)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>I consider him <em>a good friend</em>.</foreign> (NP)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>The board declared the meeting <em>a success</em>.</foreign> (NP)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <p>Object complements are most often noun phrases or adjective phrases. The nominal use—an NP renaming the direct object—is the relevant pattern for this chapter.</p>

  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 14.4  NOUN PHRASE NOMINALS                                    -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="ch-14-nominals-sec-144">
    <title>Noun Phrase Nominals</title>

    <p>The noun phrase is the prototypical nominal—the structure that fills argument positions by default. Every other nominal type in this chapter can be understood as a non-NP structure stepping into a slot that an NP would normally occupy. Understanding the NP baseline makes the other types easier to grasp, because once you know what an NP can do, you can recognize a gerund or a clause as <em>doing the same job</em>—occupying the same slot, satisfying the same valency, triggering the same verb agreement—simply through a different surface form.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Formation</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>A noun phrase consists of a head noun with optional determiners, pre-modifiers, and post-modifiers (Chapter 7). The head determines the agreement features of the whole NP:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><em>Bare head:</em> <foreign><em>books</em></foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><em>Determiner + head:</em> <foreign><em>the book</em></foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><em>Determiner + pre-modifier + head:</em> <foreign><em>the interesting book</em></foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><em>Pre-modification and post-modification combined:</em> <foreign><em>the interesting old book on the shelf</em></foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <p>Section 14.1 showed a basic NP filling the subject slot in <foreign>The students read books</foreign>. The labeling table and tree diagram for that example serve as the canonical NP-as-subject illustration; refer back to it for the simplest case. Below we illustrate a more developed NP—one with both pre-modification and post-modification—to show that internal complexity does not change the nominal function.</p>

    <p>Consider the sentence <foreign>She read the interesting book on the shelf</foreign>. The subject <foreign>She</foreign> is a pronoun NP; the direct object <foreign>the interesting book on the shelf</foreign> is a much heavier NP that combines a determiner, an adjective phrase, a head noun, and a postmodifying prepositional phrase. Despite the difference in length, both NPs do exactly what NPs do: they fill an argument slot of the verb <foreign>read</foreign>.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Labeling Table: <foreign>She read the interesting book on the shelf</foreign></title>
    </paragraphs>

    <image source="diagrams/new/ch14_table_017.png" width="95%">
      <description>Multi-level labeling table for "She read the interesting book on the shelf"</description>
    </image>
    <image source="diagrams/new/ch14_np_complex.png" width="65%">
      <description>Syntax tree for "She read the interesting book on the shelf" showing a complex NP as direct object</description>
    </image>
    <p><c>[S [NP [PRON She]] [VP [V read] [NP [DET the] [ADJP [ADJ interesting]] [N book] [PP [PREP on] [NP [DET the] [N shelf]]]]]]</c></p>

    <p>The internal structure of the direct-object NP shows three layers of modification—an adjective before the head, a prepositional phrase after the head, and a smaller NP inside that PP—but the topmost label of the whole structure is still NP, and its role is still Direct Object. That is the point: NPs scale up without changing what they are functionally.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Key Points</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <ul>
      <li><p>NPs can fill all six nominal positions (subject, direct object, indirect object, object of preposition, subject complement, object complement).</p></li>
      <li><p>The head noun determines the number (singular/plural) and triggers verb agreement, regardless of how many modifiers attach to it.</p></li>
      <li><p>Chapters 12 and 13 showed NPs functioning as adverbials and adjectivals—form is noun phrase, but function varies. Position in the sentence is what tells you whether an NP is acting as a nominal, an adverbial, or an adjectival.</p></li>
    </ul>

  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 14.5  PRONOUN NOMINALS                                        -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="ch-14-nominals-sec-145">
    <title>Pronoun Nominals</title>

    <p>Pronouns are the most compact nominals—single words that replace entire noun phrases. Chapter 6 covered pronouns as a form class. Here the focus is on their nominal function: pronouns fill the same argument positions as full NPs, but they carry information about person, number, gender, and case in a single word.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Case and Position</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>English pronouns change form based on their nominal position (case):</p>

    <tabular halign="left">
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell>Position</cell>
        <cell>Case</cell>
        <cell>Example</cell>
      </row>
      <row><cell>Subject</cell><cell>Subjective (<foreign>I, she, he, they</foreign>)</cell><cell><foreign><em>She</em> arrived.</foreign></cell></row>
      <row><cell>Direct/Indirect Object</cell><cell>Objective (<foreign>me, her, him, them</foreign>)</cell><cell><foreign>I saw <em>her</em>.</foreign></cell></row>
      <row><cell>Object of Preposition</cell><cell>Objective</cell><cell><foreign>He spoke to <em>them</em>.</foreign></cell></row>
      <row><cell>Subject Complement</cell><cell>Subjective (formal) or Objective (informal)</cell><cell><foreign>It is <em>I</em>.</foreign> / <foreign>It's <em>me</em>.</foreign></cell></row>
      <row><cell>Possessive</cell><cell>Possessive (<foreign>my/mine, her/hers</foreign>)</cell><cell><foreign><em>Her</em> book</foreign> / <foreign>The book is <em>hers</em>.</foreign></cell></row>
    </tabular>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Key Points</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <ul>
      <li><p>Pronouns are the only English nominals that change form based on their syntactic position (case marking).</p></li>
      <li><p>In formal usage, subject complements take subjective case: <foreign>It is I</foreign>. In informal speech, objective case is standard: <foreign>It's me</foreign>.</p></li>
      <li><p>See Chapter 6 for the full pronoun classification (personal, demonstrative, indefinite, relative, interrogative, reflexive).</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Labeling Table: <foreign>She gave him the book</foreign></title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>The sentence <foreign>She gave him the book</foreign> packs three nominals into a single short clause—a subject pronoun, an indirect object pronoun, and a direct object noun phrase. The labeling table below makes this triple visible:</p>

    <image source="diagrams/new/ch14_table_015.png" width="80%">
      <description>Multi-level labeling table for "She gave him the book"</description>
    </image>
    <image source="diagrams/new/ch14_pronoun_ditrans.png" width="55%">
      <description>Syntax tree for "She gave him the book" showing three pronouns and a noun phrase in subject, indirect object, and direct object positions</description>
    </image>
    <p><c>[S [NP [PRON She]] [VP [V gave] [NP [PRON him]] [NP [DET the] [N book]]]]</c></p>

    <p>Notice that all three nominals are NPs at the phrase level even though one of them is a single pronoun and another is a determiner-plus-noun. Position—not internal complexity—is what makes each nominal what it is. Notice too the case shift: <foreign>she</foreign> in subject position and <foreign>him</foreign> in object position are the same person, but English requires different forms.</p>

  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 14.6  GERUND PHRASE NOMINALS                                  -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="ch-14-nominals-sec-146">
    <title>Gerund Phrase Nominals</title>

    <p>A <term>gerund</term> is the <foreign>-ing</foreign> form of a verb functioning nominally. Gerunds present one of the clearest examples of the form-vs.-function distinction at work. Morphologically, <foreign>swimming</foreign> looks exactly like a present participle—the same <foreign>-ing</foreign> ending, the same verbal form. The difference is purely functional: when it modifies a noun (as in <foreign>the swimming dog</foreign>), it is a participle functioning adjectivally; when it fills a noun-phrase slot (as in <foreign>Swimming is good exercise</foreign>), it is a gerund functioning nominally. Because gerunds retain their verbal character, they can take their own objects and modifiers, which means a gerund phrase can be quite long even though it fills a single argument slot.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Formation</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>A <term>gerund phrase</term> consists of a gerund (the <foreign>-ing</foreign> form) plus any complements and modifiers:</p>

    <p>Gerund Phrase = (Possessive) + Gerund + (Complements/Modifiers)</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>Swimming</em> is fun.</foreign> (simple gerund)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>Swimming laps</em> is good exercise.</foreign> (gerund + object)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>His swimming</em> improved.</foreign> (possessive + gerund)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>Their constantly complaining</em> annoys everyone.</foreign> (possessive + modifier + gerund)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Gerund Functions</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p><em>As subject:</em></p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>Running marathons</em> requires dedication.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>Finishing on time</em> seemed impossible.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>As direct object:</em></p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>She enjoys <em>cooking elaborate meals</em>.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>He avoided <em>making promises</em>.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>As object of preposition:</em></p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>She succeeded by <em>working hard</em>.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>He's interested in <em>learning languages</em>.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>As subject complement:</em></p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>His favorite activity is <em>reading</em>.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>The challenge was <em>convincing the board</em>.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Possessive with Gerunds</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>Formal usage prefers possessive forms before gerunds:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>I appreciated <em>his helping</em> us.</foreign> (formal)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>I appreciated <em>him helping</em> us.</foreign> (informal)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <p>Both are acceptable in modern English, though the possessive is preferred in formal writing.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Labeling Table: <foreign>Running every morning is healthy</foreign></title>
    </paragraphs>

    <image source="diagrams/new/ch14_table_011.png" width="65%">
      <description>Multi-level labeling table for "Running every morning is healthy"</description>
    </image>
    <image source="diagrams/new/ch14_gerund_running.png" width="55%">
      <description>Syntax tree for "Running every morning is healthy" showing a gerund phrase as subject</description>
    </image>
    <p><c>[S [VP [V Running] [NP [DET every] [N morning]]] [VP [V is] [ADJP [ADJ healthy]]]]</c></p>

    <p>The gerund phrase <foreign>Running every morning</foreign> fills the subject slot—a position normally occupied by a noun phrase.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Key Points</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <ul>
      <li><p>Gerunds look identical to present participles (<foreign>-ing</foreign>). The distinction is functional: gerunds are nominals, participles are adjectivals or part of verb phrases.</p></li>
      <li><p>Gerund phrases can fill subject, direct object, indirect object, object of preposition, and subject complement positions—including object of preposition (which infinitives cannot).</p></li>
      <li><p>In diagrams, label the gerund phrase as GP (gerund phrase) at the phrase level.</p></li>
    </ul>

  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 14.7  INFINITIVE PHRASE NOMINALS                              -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="ch-14-nominals-sec-147">
    <title>Infinitive Phrase Nominals</title>

    <p>Like gerund phrases, infinitive phrases are built on a verbal base, and they can take their own objects and modifiers. In Chapter 12 you saw infinitive phrases as adverbials of purpose (<foreign>She came to help</foreign>). In Chapter 13 you saw them as adjectivals modifying nouns (<foreign>a book to read</foreign>). Here the focus is on their nominal use—when an infinitive phrase fills a subject, object, or complement position. Unlike gerunds, infinitives have a narrower distribution as nominals: they appear frequently as subjects and complements, but only certain verbs allow an infinitive direct object.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Formation</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>Infinitive Phrase = <foreign>to</foreign> + Verb + (Complements/Modifiers)</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>To swim</em> is fun.</foreign> (simple infinitive)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>To finish on time</em> seemed impossible.</foreign> (infinitive + modifier)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>To convince the board of our plan</em> took hours.</foreign> (infinitive + complex complement)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Infinitive Functions</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p><em>As subject</em> (often with extraposition):</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>To err</em> is human.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>It</em> is difficult <em>to learn a new language</em>.</foreign> (extraposed)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>As direct object:</em></p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>She wants <em>to succeed</em>.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>They decided <em>to leave early</em>.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>He hopes <em>to be promoted</em>.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>As subject complement:</em></p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>The goal is <em>to finish on time</em>.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>Her dream is <em>to travel the world</em>.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Extraposition</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>When infinitive subjects are long or complex, English often moves them to the end of the sentence and places the pronoun <foreign>it</foreign> at the front as a placeholder. This pattern is called <term>extraposition</term>:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>To explain all the details would take too long.</foreign> (full subject)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>It</em> would take too long <em>to explain all the details</em>.</foreign> (extraposed)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>To learn a new language at fifty is challenging.</foreign> (full subject)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>It</em> is challenging <em>to learn a new language at fifty</em>.</foreign> (extraposed)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <p>Extraposition is extremely common in both speech and writing. The extraposed version is often preferred because it places the main verb closer to the beginning of the sentence, making it easier to process. The labeling table below shows what extraposition looks like under analysis: the surface subject is the placeholder pronoun <foreign>it</foreign>, while the infinitive phrase—the <em>real</em> subject in semantic terms—appears at the end of the clause.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Labeling Table: <foreign>It is difficult to learn a new language</foreign> (extraposition)</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <image source="diagrams/new/ch14_table_016.png" width="90%">
      <description>Multi-level labeling table for extraposed infinitive subject "It is difficult to learn a new language"</description>
    </image>
    <image source="diagrams/new/ch14_extraposition.png" width="65%">
      <description>Syntax tree for "It is difficult to learn a new language" showing the placeholder it and the extraposed infinitive phrase</description>
    </image>
    <p><c>[S [NP [PRON It]] [VP [V is] [ADJP [ADJ difficult]] [VP [V to learn] [NP [DET a] [ADJP [ADJ new]] [N language]]]]]</c></p>

    <p>Two things stand out. First, English fills the surface subject slot with <foreign>it</foreign> even though that pronoun has no real referent—a pure placeholder. Second, the infinitive phrase is labeled <term>Extraposed Subject</term> to mark the unusual relationship: it is the underlying subject, but it has been displaced to the end of the clause to satisfy the end-weight preference. Notice also that the infinitive <foreign>to learn</foreign> sits in a single column under a single V—the marker <foreign>to</foreign> and the verb <foreign>learn</foreign> form one verbal unit.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Labeling Table: <foreign>To win the race was her only goal</foreign></title>
    </paragraphs>

    <image source="diagrams/new/ch14_table_012.png" width="65%">
      <description>Multi-level labeling table for "To win the race was her only goal"</description>
    </image>
    <image source="diagrams/new/ch14_inf_subject.png" width="55%">
      <description>Syntax tree for "To win the race was her only goal" showing an infinitive phrase as subject</description>
    </image>
    <p><c>[S [VP [V To win] [NP [DET the] [N race]]] [VP [V was] [NP [DET her] [ADJP [ADJ only]] [N goal]]]]</c></p>

    <p>The infinitive phrase <foreign>To win the race</foreign> fills the subject position. Note that the marker <foreign>to</foreign> and the verb <foreign>win</foreign> are treated as a single verb unit—they live in one cell of the labeling table and one V node in the tree. The phrase as a whole is a VP serving the nominal function (Subject); the form is verbal but the function is nominal.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Key Points</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <ul>
      <li><p>Nominal infinitives fill subject, direct object, and complement positions—but not object of preposition (use a gerund instead: <foreign>interested in learning</foreign>, not <foreign><delete>interested in to learn</delete></foreign>).</p></li>
      <li><p>Extraposition with <foreign>it</foreign> is the most common way to handle long infinitive subjects.</p></li>
      <li><p>The same infinitive form can be nominal (filling an argument slot), adverbial (expressing purpose), or adjectival (modifying a noun)—always check the position.</p></li>
    </ul>

  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 14.8  COMPLEMENT CLAUSES: THAT-CLAUSES                       -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="ch-14-nominals-sec-148">
    <title>Complement Clauses: That-Clauses</title>

    <p>A <term>complement clause</term> (also called a <em>nominal clause</em> or <em>noun clause</em>) is a full clause—with its own subject and predicate—that fills a nominal position in a larger sentence. The term <em>complement</em> here means that the clause completes the meaning of a verb, adjective, or noun. This section covers that-clauses; the next section covers wh-clauses. Together they are the two main types of complement clause in English.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Formation</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>A that-clause consists of the complementizer <foreign>that</foreign> followed by a complete clause (subject + predicate):</p>

    <p>That-clause = <foreign>that</foreign> + Subject + Predicate</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>That she won</em> surprised no one.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>I believe <em>that she is honest</em>.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Positions</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p><em>As subject:</em></p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>That she won</em> surprised no one.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>That the earth is round</em> is well established.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>As direct object</em> (<foreign>that</foreign> often omitted):</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>I believe <em>(that) she's honest</em>.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>He knows <em>(that) we're coming</em>.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>As subject complement:</em></p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>The truth is <em>that we need more time</em>.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>My hope is <em>that things will improve</em>.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>After adjectives:</em></p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>I'm glad <em>that you came</em>.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>She was certain <em>that he was wrong</em>.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Omitting <foreign>that</foreign></title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>When a that-clause is a direct object, the complementizer <foreign>that</foreign> can often be omitted with no change in meaning:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>I think <em>(that)</em> she's right.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>He said <em>(that)</em> he would come.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <p>When a that-clause is a subject, <foreign>that</foreign> cannot be omitted:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>That</em> she won surprised everyone.</foreign> (not <foreign><delete>She won surprised everyone.</delete></foreign>)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Labeling Table: <foreign>I believe that she is honest</foreign></title>
    </paragraphs>

    <image source="diagrams/new/ch14_table_013.png" width="65%">
      <description>Multi-level labeling table for "I believe that she is honest"</description>
    </image>
    <image source="diagrams/new/ch14_that_believe.png" width="55%">
      <description>Syntax tree for "I believe that she is honest" showing a that-clause as direct object</description>
    </image>
    <p><c>[S [NP [PRON I]] [VP [V believe] [CC [COMP that] [NP [PRON she]] [VP [V is] [ADJP [ADJ honest]]]]]]</c></p>

    <p>The that-clause <foreign>that she is honest</foreign> fills the direct object position after the verb <foreign>believe</foreign>. The clause has its own subject (<foreign>she</foreign>), its own verb (<foreign>is</foreign>), and its own complement (<foreign>honest</foreign>)—a complete propositional structure embedded as a single argument of the higher verb. Section 14.11 collects additional that-clause and wh-clause diagrams for reference.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Key Points</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <ul>
      <li><p>That-clauses report propositions—statements about the world that can be true or false.</p></li>
      <li><p>The complementizer <foreign>that</foreign> can be omitted in object position but not in subject position.</p></li>
      <li><p>Common verbs taking that-clause objects: <foreign>believe, know, think, say, hope, realize, admit, claim, doubt</foreign>.</p></li>
      <li><p>In diagrams, label the complementizer <foreign>that</foreign> as COMP.</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Variations: That-clause in Other Positions</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>The example above placed the that-clause in direct-object position. Two further patterns are common: the that-clause as <em>subject</em> and the that-clause as <em>subject complement</em>. Each illustrates that the same internal structure—a complementizer plus a full clause—can fill different argument slots in the matrix sentence.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Labeling Table: <foreign>That she resigned surprised everyone</foreign> (that-clause as Subject)</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <image source="diagrams/new/ch14_table_018.png" width="80%">
      <description>Multi-level labeling table for "That she resigned surprised everyone"</description>
    </image>
    <image source="diagrams/new/ch14_that_subject.png" width="55%">
      <description>Syntax tree for "That she resigned surprised everyone" showing a that-clause filling subject position</description>
    </image>
    <p><c>[S [CC [COMP That] [NP [PRON she]] [VP [V resigned]]] [VP [V surprised] [NP [PRON everyone]]]]</c></p>

    <p>The that-clause here is the subject of <foreign>surprised</foreign>. Notice that in subject position the complementizer <foreign>that</foreign> cannot be dropped—removing it would produce <foreign><delete>She resigned surprised everyone</delete></foreign>, which English does not allow.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Labeling Table: <foreign>The truth is that she lied</foreign> (that-clause as Subject Complement)</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <image source="diagrams/new/ch14_table_019.png" width="80%">
      <description>Multi-level labeling table for "The truth is that she lied"</description>
    </image>
    <image source="diagrams/new/ch14_that_complement.png" width="55%">
      <description>Syntax tree for "The truth is that she lied" showing a that-clause as subject complement after the linking verb</description>
    </image>
    <p><c>[S [NP [DET The] [N truth]] [VP [V is] [CC [COMP that] [NP [PRON she]] [VP [V lied]]]]]</c></p>

    <p>Here the that-clause follows the linking verb <foreign>is</foreign> and identifies what the subject <foreign>The truth</foreign> consists of. Pedagogically this pattern is worth memorizing because it is the most common way to deliver a propositional definition or explanation in formal writing.</p>

  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 14.9  COMPLEMENT CLAUSES: WH-CLAUSES                         -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="ch-14-nominals-sec-149">
    <title>Complement Clauses: Wh-Clauses</title>

    <p>The second major type of complement clause is the <term>wh-clause</term> (also called an <em>interrogative content clause</em> or <em>indirect question</em>). While that-clauses embed statements, wh-clauses embed questions. In <foreign>I know that she left</foreign>, the speaker states a fact; in <foreign>I know where she went</foreign>, the speaker reports the answer to an implicit question. The wh-word simultaneously introduces the clause and fills a grammatical role within it—much like a relative pronoun does in a relative clause (Chapter 13), but here the clause functions nominally rather than adjectivally.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Formation</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>A wh-clause consists of a wh-word followed by clause content. The wh-word both introduces the clause and fills a role within it:</p>

    <p>Wh-clause = Wh-word + (Subject) + Predicate</p>

    <tabular halign="left">
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell>Wh-word</cell>
        <cell>Asks about</cell>
        <cell>Example</cell>
      </row>
      <row><cell><foreign>what</foreign></cell><cell>things/actions</cell><cell><foreign>I know <em>what happened</em>.</foreign></cell></row>
      <row><cell><foreign>who/whom</foreign></cell><cell>people</cell><cell><foreign>She asked <em>who called</em>.</foreign></cell></row>
      <row><cell><foreign>which</foreign></cell><cell>specific choice</cell><cell><foreign>Tell me <em>which one you prefer</em>.</foreign></cell></row>
      <row><cell><foreign>where</foreign></cell><cell>place</cell><cell><foreign>I don't know <em>where he went</em>.</foreign></cell></row>
      <row><cell><foreign>when</foreign></cell><cell>time</cell><cell><foreign>She asked <em>when we would arrive</em>.</foreign></cell></row>
      <row><cell><foreign>why</foreign></cell><cell>reason</cell><cell><foreign>I wonder <em>why she left</em>.</foreign></cell></row>
      <row><cell><foreign>how</foreign></cell><cell>manner/extent</cell><cell><foreign>Tell me <em>how you did it</em>.</foreign></cell></row>
      <row><cell><foreign>whether/if</foreign></cell><cell>yes/no</cell><cell><foreign>I wonder <em>whether she'll come</em>.</foreign></cell></row>
    </tabular>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Positions</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p><em>As subject:</em></p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>What she discovered</em> changed everything.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>Where they went</em> remains a mystery.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>Whether he'll accept</em> is uncertain.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>As direct object:</em></p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>I don't know <em>what happened</em>.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>She asked <em>whether I was coming</em>.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>Tell me <em>how you did it</em>.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>As subject complement:</em></p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>The issue is <em>how we proceed</em>.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>The question is <em>whether to accept</em>.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>If vs. Whether</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>Both <foreign>if</foreign> and <foreign>whether</foreign> introduce yes/no complement clauses, but they are not always interchangeable:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>Whether</foreign> can be a subject; <foreign>if</foreign> typically cannot:</p></li>
    </ul>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>Whether</em> he comes remains to be seen.</foreign> ✓</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><delete>If he comes remains to be seen.</delete></foreign> ✗</p></li>
    </ul>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>Whether</foreign> allows <foreign>or not</foreign> immediately after; <foreign>if</foreign> does not:</p></li>
    </ul>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>I don't know <em>whether or not</em> she's coming.</foreign> ✓</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><delete>I don't know if or not she's coming.</delete></foreign> ✗</p></li>
    </ul>

    <p>In general, <foreign>whether</foreign> is preferred in formal writing and required in subject position.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Labeling Table: <foreign>She asked whether we could help</foreign></title>
    </paragraphs>

    <image source="diagrams/new/ch14_table_014.png" width="65%">
      <description>Multi-level labeling table for "She asked whether we could help"</description>
    </image>
    <image source="diagrams/new/ch14_whether_asked.png" width="55%">
      <description>Syntax tree for "She asked whether we could help" showing a whether-clause as direct object</description>
    </image>
    <p><c>[S [NP [PRON She]] [VP [V asked] [CC [COMP whether] [NP [PRON we]] [VP [MOD could] [V help]]]]]</c></p>

    <p>The wh-word <foreign>whether</foreign> introduces the embedded clause and signals that the speaker is reporting an unresolved yes/no question rather than asserting a fact. Compare this with the parallel <foreign>if</foreign>-clause and additional examples in Section 14.11.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Key Points</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <ul>
      <li><p>Wh-clauses embed questions as nominals. The word order within the clause is declarative, not interrogative: <foreign>I know where she went</foreign> (not <foreign><delete>I know where did she go</delete></foreign>).</p></li>
      <li><p>Use <foreign>whether</foreign> (not <foreign>if</foreign>) in subject position and before <foreign>or not</foreign>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Common verbs taking wh-clause objects: <foreign>know, wonder, ask, tell, explain, decide, understand</foreign>.</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Variations: Wh-word Roles and Wh-clause Positions</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>Wh-clauses are richer than that-clauses in two ways. First, the wh-word can itself fill a grammatical role inside the embedded clause—as subject, object, or even adverbial. Second, wh-clauses are the only complement-clause type that can serve as the object of a preposition. The two examples below illustrate each variation.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Labeling Table: <foreign>What happened was unexpected</foreign> (wh-word as embedded subject)</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <image source="diagrams/new/ch14_table_020.png" width="65%">
      <description>Multi-level labeling table for "What happened was unexpected" showing the wh-word as the subject of its own clause</description>
    </image>
    <image source="diagrams/new/ch14_wh_embedded_subject.png" width="50%">
      <description>Syntax tree for "What happened was unexpected" showing the wh-word filling the subject NP slot of the embedded clause</description>
    </image>
    <p><c>[S [CC [NP [PRON What]] [VP [V happened]]] [VP [V was] [ADJP [ADJ unexpected]]]]</c></p>

    <p>Here the wh-word <foreign>What</foreign> serves a double duty: it introduces the embedded clause <em>and</em> functions as that clause's subject. There is no separate complementizer node—the wh-pronoun fills the subject NP slot directly. The whole CC then occupies the matrix subject slot.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Labeling Table: <foreign>We talked about what he said</foreign> (wh-clause as Object of Preposition)</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <image source="diagrams/new/ch14_table_021.png" width="80%">
      <description>Multi-level labeling table for "We talked about what he said" showing a wh-clause as object of preposition</description>
    </image>
    <image source="diagrams/new/ch14_wh_objprep.png" width="55%">
      <description>Syntax tree for "We talked about what he said" showing the wh-clause filling the object-of-preposition slot inside an adverbial PP</description>
    </image>
    <p><c>[S [NP [PRON We]] [VP [V talked] [PP [PREP about] [CC [COMP what] [NP [PRON he]] [VP [V said]]]]]]</c></p>

    <p>Of all the complement-clause types, only wh-clauses can sit after a preposition. You can say <foreign>about what he said</foreign> but not <foreign><delete>about that he said</delete></foreign>. This pattern is common after verbs and adjectives that select prepositional complements: <foreign>think about, talk about, interested in, curious about, worried about</foreign>.</p>

  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 14.10 (CUT) COMPARING NOMINAL STRUCTURES                      -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="ch-14-nominals-sec-1410">
    <title>Comparing Nominal Structures</title>

    <p>Different nominal structures may seem interchangeable but often carry subtle differences in meaning or style. If you can say <foreign>I like swimming</foreign> and also <foreign>I like to swim</foreign>, you might wonder whether the choice of gerund versus infinitive ever matters. The answer is: sometimes no, sometimes yes, and sometimes the choice changes the meaning of the sentence entirely. This section gives you the tools to choose among nominal structures deliberately rather than by habit.</p>

    <p>Before diving into the contrasts, the table below summarizes the distributional differences among the four most common verbal/clausal nominal forms. Use it as a navigational aid: whenever a verb takes an object, the table tells you which forms are even available before you start choosing among them on stylistic grounds.</p>

    <table xml:id="ch-14-comparison">
      <title>Distributional comparison of nominal forms</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="minor" left="minor">
        <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell>Form</cell>
          <cell>Subject</cell>
          <cell>Direct Object</cell>
          <cell>Obj. of Prep.</cell>
          <cell>Subject Compl.</cell>
          <cell>Object Compl.</cell>
          <cell>Typical flavor</cell>
        </row>
        <row><cell>Gerund phrase</cell><cell>yes</cell><cell>yes</cell><cell>yes</cell><cell>yes</cell><cell>rare</cell><cell>activity / process</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Infinitive phrase</cell><cell>yes (often extraposed)</cell><cell>yes (selected verbs)</cell><cell>no</cell><cell>yes</cell><cell>rare</cell><cell>goal / hypothetical</cell></row>
        <row><cell>That-clause</cell><cell>yes</cell><cell>yes (selected verbs)</cell><cell>no</cell><cell>yes</cell><cell>no</cell><cell>asserted proposition</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Wh-clause</cell><cell>yes</cell><cell>yes (selected verbs)</cell><cell>yes</cell><cell>yes</cell><cell>no</cell><cell>embedded question</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>Three patterns are worth memorizing from this table. First, only gerunds (and wh-clauses) survive as objects of prepositions. Second, infinitive subjects almost always extrapose when they grow long—the bare-subject form is grammatical but rare in connected prose. Third, every form except gerunds is restricted to particular verbs in object position; you have to know which verbs select which complements.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Gerund vs. Infinitive</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>Many verbs take either gerund or infinitive objects with little difference:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>I like <em>swimming</em>.</foreign> / <foreign>I like <em>to swim</em>.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>She started <em>working</em>.</foreign> / <foreign>She started <em>to work</em>.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <p>But some verbs show clear differences:</p>

    <p><em>Gerund only:</em> <foreign>enjoy, avoid, finish, deny, consider, keep, mind</foreign></p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>She enjoys <em>swimming</em>.</foreign> (not <foreign><delete>to swim</delete></foreign>)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>Infinitive only:</em> <foreign>want, hope, decide, agree, refuse, plan, promise</foreign></p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>She wants <em>to swim</em>.</foreign> (not <foreign><delete>swimming</delete></foreign>)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>Different meanings with gerund vs. infinitive:</em></p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>I stopped <em>smoking</em>.</foreign> (quit the habit)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>I stopped <em>to smoke</em>.</foreign> (paused in order to smoke)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>I remember <em>locking</em> the door.</foreign> (I recall the past act of locking)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>I remember <em>to lock</em> the door.</foreign> (I don't forget to do it)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>That-clause vs. Infinitive</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>Some verbs allow both:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>I believe <em>that she's honest</em>.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>I believe <em>her to be honest</em>.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <p>The infinitive construction may sound more formal.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>That-clause vs. Gerund</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>Some verbs take either:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>He admitted <em>that he was wrong</em>.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>He admitted <em>being wrong</em>.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <p>The gerund is often more concise.</p>

  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- CHAPTER SUMMARY                                               -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="ch-14-summary">
    <title>Chapter Summary</title>

    <p>This chapter introduced nominals—structures that fill the argument positions of a sentence. The key points are:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><em>Form vs. function:</em> A noun is a part of speech (form); a nominal is any structure functioning in a noun-phrase position (function). All nouns in subject or object position are nominals, but not all nominals are nouns.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Six nominal positions:</em> subject, direct object, indirect object, object of a preposition, subject complement, and object complement. These are the argument slots that the verb requires or the sentence structure provides.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Six nominal forms:</em> noun phrases, pronouns, gerund phrases, infinitive phrases, that-clauses, and wh-clauses can each fill nominal positions, though some have distributional restrictions.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Gerund phrases</em> (<foreign>-ing</foreign> form) can fill subject, direct object, indirect object, object of a preposition, and subject complement positions.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Infinitive phrases</em> (<foreign>to</foreign> + verb) fill subject, direct object, and complement positions—but not object of a preposition.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Complement clauses</em> are full clauses filling nominal positions. That-clauses embed statements; wh-clauses embed questions. The complementizer <foreign>that</foreign> can be omitted in object position but not in subject position.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Extraposition</em> moves long subject clauses to the end of the sentence, placing <foreign>it</foreign> as a placeholder subject—a common pattern in both speech and writing.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Choice matters:</em> Gerund vs. infinitive can change meaning (<foreign>stopped smoking</foreign> vs. <foreign>stopped to smoke</foreign>), and some verbs require one or the other.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

